  "@type": "Dataset",
  "name": "Deep-ocean Assessment and Reporting of Tsunamis (DART) (PMEL Tsunami)",
  "description": "Six DART systems currently provide coverage: three stations near the seismically active Alaska-Aleutian Seismic Zone (AASZ), which has a long history of tsunamigenic earthquakes; two stations off the Washington-Oregon coast near the Cascadia Subduction Zone (CSZ); and a sixth station in the eastern equatorial region to measure tsunamis which may be generated in the Peru-Chile Seismic Zone and propagate into the north Pacific. DART systems utilize a bottom pressure recorder (BPR) to measure low period sea level phenomena such as the tides and tsunamis, an acoustic link to transmit the BPR data to a surface buoy, and satellite telemetry to relay the data to shore. DART was developed by NOAA'S NOAA's Pacific Marine Environmental Laboratory (PMEL) and is operated by NOAA's National Data Buoy Center (NDBC). PMEL DART program website - http://www.pmel.noaa.gov/tsunami/Dart/ Real-time data are available on the National Data Buoy Center website http://www.ndbc.noaa.gov/dart.shtml\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: To provide deep-ocean tsunami measurements in real-time during an actual event.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23569243",
